  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 135 PM Monday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...  Dry and seasonable weather continues through the \nweek. The first day of November could bring some rain.\n\nA continued stretch of dry and quiet weather is expected through the \nend of October. High pressure ridging will continue to dominate the \nweather pattern. Daily high temperatures each day are forecast to \nhover in the 70s through the period. The warmest days are expected \nThursday through Saturday, where some locales could rise to around \n80 degrees, especially on Friday across the southern Piedmont, \nSandhills, and Coastal Plain.\n\nRain chances could arrives as early as the start of November, during \nthe period Sunday into Monday. Models and ensembles appear to \nshowing the remnants of tropical storm Vance, presently out over the \ntropical Pacific, reaching the TN valley and southeast US early next \nweek. However, there is still a lot of uncertainty with respect to \nrainfall amounts, if any. The machine learning guidance is much \ndrier, showing a flatter quasi-zonal shortwave tracking through, \nwhile some GEFS/GFS members are wetter with a more developed wave of \nlow pressure. At this stage, confidence in expected rain early next \nweek is low. If this trends dry, the next best chance would have to \nwait until late next week.",
